I've found that, now that I pay attention to how my stomach
feels when I think about eating chocolate, I eat it much less
often....I still like the taste, but a lot of the time (not
always) the stuff actually leaves me feeling queasy, and
I generally give my innards the deciding vote. 

I don't know if this would work for you, but it's an alternate
strategy that may be of some interest. Also, I'm inclined to
think that people have probably got fairly good instincts
about what's good for them if they don't override them with
cultural myths like chocolate always being a pleasure. 
>
>Furthermore, "baby hunger" can be satiated by much more 
>immediate and accessible feedback, in the form of adopting and 
>caring for a cute pet or even a cuddly toy.  Thus "baby hunger" 

Are pets and toys always adequate substitutes?

>can be short circuited, just as birth control short circuits 
>the connection between heterosexual desire and making babies.
>
>Finally, I again refer folks to the demographic literature,
>which shows quite clearly and overwhelmingly the self-genocide
>phenomenon.  Every significant culture that has access to, doesn't
>discourage the use of, and is skilled in the use of effective
>birth control has substantially negative population growth rate,
>without exception, across a wide variety cultural origins and
>other cultural patterns.  Whatever "baby hunger" there might be 
