This reminds me of a notion--because of the high costs of raising
children in a technological culture and the availability of
other pastimes, the trend is that the only people who are
having children are those who really want to--I believe that
these will be either the best or the worst parents, with the
moderately interested middle range dropping out. I'm still
trying to figure out what the results of _that_ might be. 
>
By the way, I'm still a little weirded out by this "baby hunger"
thing....I just don't feel it. I'm 41, and while I've gotten
gradually more interested in such random babies as cross my
path (and I suspect that there's a biological basis, though
I'm also more interested in people in general than I used to be),
I haven't even reached the point of wanting to take care of a
baby for a whole afternoon, let alone having my own child.

Furthermore, I know a lot of women without children who at
least haven't mentioned wanting any. I suspect that baby
hunger isn't all that universal, though I can also believe
that it happens.

