From: "Peter C. McCluskey" <pcm@rahul.net>
Date: Mon, 17 Oct 1994 18:41:37 -0700
Subject: [#94-10-286] Reply to challenged uploaders

 EdRegis@aol.com (EdRegis@aol.com) writes in X-Message-Number: #94-10-260:
>Some wrote that everything hinges on a proper definition of consciousness.
> ("How can we have a clear objective test for something if we can't even
>define it?")  Actually, I view the matter of definition as irrelevant to the
>issue.  We know what consciousness is through personal experience, and we
>know enough about it, on the basis of that experience, to conclude that
>apples, for example, are not conscious, even though we cannot reliably define
>either "apple" (I can't, anyway) or "consciousness."  

 If the definition is irrelevant, then I can easily show how software
can have consciousness. Just have it record all its inputs and all its
high level actions, and analyze the interesting portions of those records.
That captures the experiences that I refer to as consciousness.

 The definition of consciousness doesn't need to be "reliable" to be useful.
I can figure out what "apple" means by seeing a few examples of things that
are apples and things that are not apples. People such as Dennett or Jaynes
who write clearly about consciousness describe the mental phenomena they are
interested in well enough that I can recognize those phenomena in my mind,
even when I can't objectively measure them.
 I think you have more of a definition than you have described so far. My
guess is that it resembles what AI critics call qualia (such as the experience
I get when I see the color red). Given that I can prove via tests for color
blindness that I don't experience the same thing as the average human, and
see no evidence that I would be hurt if I my experiences of the color red
were replaced by someone else's experiences of the color red, I don't worry
much about preserving my qualia during uploading.
